From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 21:59:30 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09110/11362] The ARC selection loop closed: first automated
 A/B refused a flag that loses ft09

End-to-end proof that REQ-ARC-BENCH-6267 and REQ-ARC-FLAG-LEDGER-6268 work
together, plus a diagnosis for the sc25 anomaly the baseline surfaced.

CARNOT_ARC_SMALL_OBJECT_FIRST -- REFUSED. Its source comment read "the SUBMITTED
agent is unchanged until an A/B greenlights it" (REQ-ARC-FCP-5758). The A/B had
never run. It ran now:

  baseline  8 levels   364,730 actions
  arm       7 levels   364,468 actions

The flag loses ft09, a game the baseline clears, and makes lp85, su15 and vc33
more expensive. Total actions move 0.07 percent, so reading the aggregate alone
would have called it neutral and shipped it. The per-game rule caught the lost
game -- the exact failure the regression gate was written for, firing on real
data rather than a fixture, on the first measurement ever taken.

Not a verdict on the idea, which may still be right for the games it was designed
around. A verdict on turning it on globally, recorded with evidence instead of
left open for another six months.

SC25's 24-ACTION COLLAPSE, diagnosed. At the default state key every action
dedups to an already-seen state, the frontier empties, and search exits after 24
steps while every other unsolved game spends 8,000 to 75,000. Measured at 1500
expansions: unset 24 steps, SUFFIX_K=1 4,463 steps, K=2 and K=4 3,876. The game
is not hard, it is unexplorable.

Still clears no level, so the rule correctly does not promote it -- "explores
more" is neither a level nor an efficiency gain, and rewarding extra actions
would invert the scored metric. The fix belongs in the state key, not a flag: a
key that collapses distinct states makes sc25 invisible to every future
measurement, because no flag can change an outcome decided before search runs.
